<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
        Ostler WhatsApp keepalive LaunchAgent.

        WhatsApp Web (wa-rs) sockets can drift to disconnected. Without
        something that notices and acts, the socket can die between the
        last incoming message and the next scheduled brief (09:00
        morning, 18:00 evening), so the brief's deliver_announcement
        races against an unready channel and gets silently dropped.

        WHAT THIS PLIST USED TO DO, AND WHY IT WAS CHANGED
        --------------------------------------------------
        ProgramArguments here used to be

            OSTLER_BIN/ostler-assistant  channel  doctor

        and the comment in this file used to claim that the WhatsApp arm
        of `channel doctor` "exercises the socket and triggers an
        automatic reconnect on a stale connection. Side effect we want".

        THERE IS NO SUCH SIDE EFFECT, and the probe could not pass even
        when the channel was perfectly healthy. Measured in the
        ostler-assistant source rather than inferred:

          * `channel doctor` runs in its OWN short-lived process and
            calls collect_configured_channels(), which CONSTRUCTS a
            fresh WhatsAppWebChannel. Its health_check() delegates to
            is_ready(), whose entire body is
            `self.client.lock().is_some()`. A freshly constructed
            channel has never connected, so that field is None. Upstream
            has a test called `is_ready_is_false_before_client_connects`
            asserting exactly this. The WhatsApp arm therefore reported
            UNHEALTHY on 100% of runs, on every box.

          * is_ready() is a pure read of a struct field. Nothing is
            exercised, nothing reconnects.

          * doctor_channels() counts the unhealthy channels, prints a
            summary and returns Ok(()) unconditionally, so the job
            exited 0 every time.

        On the box this was caught on, launchctl showed runs=3 and
        `last exit code = 0` while the job's own log said WhatsApp was
        unhealthy on all three runs -- and whatsapp-bundle.log showed
        that same day's messages being ingested, 13 chats scanned and 12
        dispatched. The channel was fine. The probe was measuring an
        object it had just built itself.

        WHAT IT DOES NOW
        ----------------
        It runs the keepalive script, which asks the RUNNING daemon's
        own health registry (GET /health, public on loopback, key
        `channel:WhatsApp`) -- the only surface in the product that can
        tell a live socket from a dead one. When that surface says the
        channel is in error, the script restarts the assistant
        LaunchAgent, which is the fix a person would otherwise do by
        hand, then re-checks and reports the outcome to the Doctor page.

        It NEVER re-pairs, logs the customer out, or touches the
        WhatsApp session store. When the session is genuinely gone it
        says so once and stops, rather than retrying something only the
        customer's phone can finish. See the script header for the full
        reasoning and the exit-code contract.

        The installer renders the placeholders below at install time:
          OSTLER_KEEPALIVE_SCRIPT_VALUE -> $OSTLER_DIR/bin/ostler-whatsapp-keepalive.sh
          OSTLER_ARTEFACT_ROOT_VALUE    -> $OSTLER_DIR
          OSTLER_GATEWAY_URL_VALUE      -> the daemon gateway base URL
          OSTLER_ASSISTANT_LABEL_VALUE  -> the assistant LaunchAgent label
          OSTLER_HOME                   -> $HOME (working directory)
          OSTLER_LOGS                   -> $LOGS_DIR

        British English throughout.
    -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.creativemachines.ostler.whatsapp-keepalive</string>

    <!--
        /bin/bash explicitly rather than relying on the script's execute
        bit: a plist that execs a file whose mode did not survive the
        install would thrash and log "no such file" forever, which is
        the failure mode this repo already refuses for the daemon
        binary.
    -->
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>OSTLER_KEEPALIVE_SCRIPT_VALUE</string>
    </array>

    <!--
        The script resolves everything from these. They are rendered,
        not defaulted, so the job cannot quietly probe a different
        gateway or a different artefact root than the one this install
        actually uses.
    -->
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>OSTLER_DIR</key>
        <string>OSTLER_ARTEFACT_ROOT_VALUE</string>
        <key>OSTLER_GATEWAY_URL</key>
        <string>OSTLER_GATEWAY_URL_VALUE</string>
        <key>OSTLER_ASSISTANT_LABEL</key>
        <string>OSTLER_ASSISTANT_LABEL_VALUE</string>
    </dict>

    <!--
        Two scheduled fires per day:
          - 08:50 local: 10 minutes before the morning brief (09:00).
          - 17:50 local: 10 minutes before the evening wrap (18:00).

        StartCalendarInterval honours the user's local timezone
        (launchd reads the system zone). The cron jobs in
        config.toml carry an explicit `tz` field; the keepalive
        plist matches the system zone implicitly. Both align in
        practice because the install captures USER_TZ from
        /etc/localtime in Phase 1.3.

        Two separate dicts (NOT one with multiple hour entries)
        because launchd treats StartCalendarInterval values as
        wildcards: a single dict with hour=8,minute=50 fires once,
        a dict with both hours combined would fire on every match
        of every field.

        Ten minutes is comfortably more than the observed wa-rs
        reconnect time (about 60s), so a repair attempted at 08:50 has
        finished well before the 09:00 brief asks to deliver.
    -->
    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>Hour</key>
            <integer>8</integer>
            <key>Minute</key>
            <integer>50</integer>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>Hour</key>
            <integer>17</integer>
            <key>Minute</key>
            <integer>50</integer>
        </dict>
    </array>

    <!--
        RunAtLoad: false. We do NOT want the keepalive firing on
        every login or post-install reload. It can now restart the
        assistant daemon, so a RunAtLoad fire during install would race
        the installer's own deferred daemon start. Trigger only at the
        two scheduled times.
    -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <!--
        Working directory: HOME so any relative paths resolve against
        the user's home rather than /.
    -->
    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>OSTLER_HOME</string>

    <!--
        Stdout and stderr go to $LOGS_DIR. A few lines per run: what was
        asked, what the daemon's registry said, what was done about it.

        THE LOG IS NO LONGER THE ONLY PLACE THE ANSWER GOES, and that is
        the point of the change. A verdict written only to a file nobody
        opens is how three consecutive unhealthy runs went unnoticed. The
        script also writes ~/.ostler/state/whatsapp_keepalive.json, which
        the Doctor dashboard renders as a tile, and it exits non-zero on
        any outcome that is not "the channel is up" -- so `launchctl
        print` stops reading as healthy when it is not.
    -->
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>OSTLER_LOGS/whatsapp-keepalive.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>OSTLER_LOGS/whatsapp-keepalive.err</string>

    <!--
        Background priority: keepalive is not latency-critical and
        we don't want it competing with foreground work for I/O on
        the user's daily-driver Mac. Matches the wiki-recompile
        agent's pick.
    -->
    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>

    <key>Nice</key>
    <integer>5</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
